window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-struct-org-animals","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Structural Organisation in Animals · Practice","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a female frog, the ovaries have no functional connection with the kidneys.\nReason (R): In a female frog, the oviducts open into the ureters before reaching the cloaca.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the ovaries lie near the kidneys but have no functional connection with them.\nR is false. The oviducts open into the cloaca separately; they do not join the ureters. In females, the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In a frog, the circulation of blood throughout the body is achieved by:","image":"","options":["The pumping action of the muscular heart","Diffusion of blood through the moist skin","Contraction of the limb muscles during leaping","The pumping action of lymph nodes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the circulation of blood is achieved by the pumping action of the muscular heart.\nThe skin is a site of gas exchange, not the cause of blood flow.\nLimb muscles and lymph nodes do not drive the circulation of blood.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines a frog specimen. She finds a thickened pad on the first digit of the limbs that bear only four digits. She also notices sound-producing sacs. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a female; the pad is on the first digit of the hind limb","It is a male; the pad is the copulatory pad on the first digit of the hind limb","It is a female; the sacs are vocal sacs present in both sexes","It is a male; the pad is the copulatory pad on the first digit of the forelimb"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The limbs with four digits are the forelimbs, because the hind limbs have five digits.\nA copulatory pad on the first digit of the forelimb is found only in males.\nVocal sacs are also present only in males, which is an example of sexual dimorphism.\nOption (2) names the wrong limb.\nOption (3) is wrong because females lack vocal sacs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose the common bile duct of a frog becomes completely blocked. Based on NCERT's description of frog digestion, which outcome is most expected?","image":"","options":["Formation of chyme in the stomach would stop, because HCl secretion depends on bile","Only emulsification of fats would be affected, since pancreatic juice reaches the duodenum by a separate route","Both emulsification of fats and pancreatic digestion of carbohydrates and proteins in the duodenum would be impaired","Only absorption by the villi would be affected, while all digestion would continue normally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bile and pancreatic juice both reach the duodenum through the common bile duct. A block would therefore stop both from arriving.\nWithout bile, fats are not emulsified. Without pancreatic juice, carbohydrates and proteins are not digested in the duodenum.\nOption (1) fails: chyme forms in the stomach by HCl and gastric juice, which do not depend on bile.\nOption (2) fails: NCERT describes a common route for both juices.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the male frog, the ureter functions as a urinogenital duct.\nReason (R): The vasa efferentia from the testes enter the kidney and open into Bidder's canal, which communicates with the duct coming out of the kidney.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In males, the ureter carries both urine and sperms to the cloaca.\nR is true. There are 10–12 vasa efferentia. They arise from the testes, enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal, which communicates with the urinogenital duct.\nR explains A: because sperms reach the ureter through the kidney, the ureter becomes a shared urinary and genital passage.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The development of a frog includes a larval stage that changes into the adult by metamorphosis. This larva is called a:","image":"","options":["Nymph","Tadpole","Caterpillar","Planula"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: frog development involves a larval stage called the tadpole, which undergoes metamorphosis to form the adult.\nA nymph is the young stage of certain insects.\nA caterpillar is the larva of a butterfly or moth.\nA planula is a cnidarian larva.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, how many ova can a mature female frog lay at a time?","image":"","options":["250 to 300","2500 to 3000","25000 to 30000","500 to 1000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that a mature female frog can lay 2500 to 3000 ova at a time.\nFertilisation is external and takes place in water, so many eggs are lost. A large number of ova helps make sure some survive.\nThe other options are either ten times too small or ten times too large.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Venous blood returning from the body enters the frog's heart and finally leaves the ventricle. Which sequence correctly traces its path?","image":"","options":["Vena cava → right atrium → sinus venosus → ventricle → conus arteriosus","Vena cava → sinus venosus → right atrium → ventricle → conus arteriosus","Vena cava → sinus venosus → ventricle → right atrium → conus arteriosus","Vena cava → conus arteriosus → right atrium → ventricle → sinus venosus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: The major veins (vena cava) deliver blood to the sinus venosus.\nStep 2: The sinus venosus joins the right atrium, so blood passes into it next.\nStep 3: Both atria lead into the single ventricle.\nStep 4: The ventricle opens into the conus arteriosus on the ventral side.\nOption (1) wrongly places the sinus venosus after the atrium. Option (4) puts the conus arteriosus on the receiving side.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, all complex animals are built from how many basic types of tissues?","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that all complex animals consist of only four basic types of tissues: epithelial, connective, muscular and neural.\nOrgans such as the stomach, lung, heart and kidney form when these four tissues are arranged in a specific proportion and pattern.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The diencephalon of the frog brain is a paired structure of the fore-brain.\nStatement II: The cerebral hemispheres of the frog brain are paired structures of the fore-brain.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: NCERT describes the diencephalon as an unpaired part of the forebrain.\nStatement II is correct: the cerebral hemispheres are paired and belong to the forebrain.\nThe olfactory lobes are also part of the forebrain.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"While dissecting a frog, a student sees fine ductules running from the gonads into the kidneys. She also finds that the only ducts carrying gonadal products to the cloaca are the ducts emerging from the kidneys. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["The frog is female, and ova reach the cloaca through Bidder's canal","The frog is male, and its ureters carry both urine and sperms to the cloaca","The frog is female, and the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca","The frog is male, and the sperms reach the cloaca through ducts that bypass the kidneys"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ductules running from the gonads into the kidneys are the vasa efferentia. These are present only in males, so the frog is male.\nThe vasa efferentia open into Bidder's canal, which joins the urinogenital duct (ureter). This duct opens into the cloaca.\nSo the ureter carries both urine and sperms.\nIn females, the ovaries have no functional connection with the kidneys, which rules out options (1) and (3).\nOption (4) is wrong because sperms pass through the kidney, not around it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for the circulatory system of frog.\nList-I:\nA. Sinus venosus\nB. Conus arteriosus\nC. Hepatic portal system\nD. Renal portal system\nList-II:\n(i) Venous connection between the kidney and the lower parts of the body\n(ii) Triangular structure joining the right atrium and receiving the vena cava\n(iii) Sac-like structure into which the ventricle opens on the ventral side\n(iv) Venous connection between the liver and the intestine\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): The sinus venosus is triangular, joins the right atrium and receives blood through the vena cava.\nB-(iii): The ventricle opens into the sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side.\nC-(iv): The hepatic portal system links the liver and the intestine.\nD-(i): The renal portal system links the kidney and the lower body parts.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with reference to digestion in frog.\nList-I\n(a) Bile\n(b) Pancreatic juice\n(c) HCl and gastric juices\n(d) Villi and microvilli\nList-II\n(i) Absorption of digested food\n(ii) Emulsification of fats\n(iii) Digestion of carbohydrates and proteins\n(iv) Digestion in stomach forming chyme","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Bile emulsifies fat, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Pancreatic juice digests carbohydrates and proteins, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) HCl and gastric juices from the stomach wall digest food into partially digested chyme, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) Villi and microvilli are finger-like folds of the intestinal wall that absorb digested food, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose frogs were completely removed from a paddy-field ecosystem. Based on the ecological role of frogs given in NCERT, which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Insect populations would rise, increasing crop damage and disturbing the food web","Insect populations would fall because frogs compete with insects for food","Crop yield would rise because frogs feed on rice plants","The food web would remain unaffected because frogs are only consumers at the top"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Frogs eat insects and so protect crops. Removing them removes a check on insect numbers.\nMore insects would mean more crop damage.\nFrogs are also an important link in food chains and food webs. They are prey for other animals, not only top consumers, so their loss would disturb the ecological balance.\nFrogs do not eat rice plants, and they do not compete with insects for food.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The lymph of a frog differs from its blood because lymph lacks:","image":"","options":["WBCs and plasma","Water and nutrients","RBCs and a few proteins","All proteins and all WBCs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says lymph is different from blood: it lacks a few proteins and the RBCs.\nLymph is still a fluid, so it has a plasma-like liquid part and water. Options (1) and (2) are therefore wrong.\nOnly some proteins are missing, not all of them. So option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a male frog, the ureter carries both urine and sperms and acts as a urinogenital duct.\nReason (R): In a female frog, the ovaries have no functional connection with the kidneys, and the oviducts open separately into the cloaca.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In males, the vasa efferentia send sperms through the kidney via Bidder's canal. So the ureter carries both urine and sperms and acts as a urinogenital duct.\nR is true. In females, the ovaries are only near the kidneys and have no functional link with them. The oviducts open into the cloaca separately from the ureters.\nR describes the female condition. It does not explain why the male ureter is urinogenital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a male frog, the ureter carries urine as well as sperms and is therefore called the urinogenital duct.\nStatement II: In a female frog, the ureters and the oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In males, sperms pass from the vasa efferentia into Bidder's canal in the kidney, which communicates with the ureter. The ureter therefore carries both urine and sperms and acts as the urinogenital duct.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that in females the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca. The ovaries have no functional connection with the kidneys.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Each kidney of a frog is made up of several structural and functional units. These units are called","image":"","options":["Bidder's canals","uriniferous tubules or nephrons","vasa efferentia","villi"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: each kidney is composed of several structural and functional units called uriniferous tubules, or nephrons.\nBidder's canal lies inside the kidney of the male frog. It receives the vasa efferentia from the testes, so it carries sperms and is not the excretory unit.\nThe vasa efferentia themselves arise from the testes.\nVilli are folds of the intestine that absorb digested food.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with reference to the nervous system of the frog:\nList-I\nA. Optic lobes\nB. Medulla oblongata\nC. Diencephalon\nD. Cranium\nList-II\n(i) Part of hind-brain\n(ii) Bony brain box enclosing the brain\n(iii) Characteristic of mid-brain\n(iv) Unpaired part of fore-brain\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(iii): A pair of optic lobes characterises the mid-brain.\nB-(i): The medulla oblongata, together with the cerebellum, forms the hind-brain. It passes out through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord.\nC-(iv): The fore-brain has olfactory lobes, paired cerebral hemispheres and an unpaired diencephalon.\nD-(ii): The brain is enclosed in a bony brain box called the cranium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In frog, chemical coordination of the various organs of the body is achieved by","image":"","options":["nerve impulses carried by spinal nerves","hormones secreted by endocrine glands","digestive enzymes secreted by the pancreas","lymph circulating through lymph nodes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The frog's control and coordination system has two parts: the neural system and the endocrine glands.\nChemical coordination is brought about by hormones secreted by the endocrine glands.\nNerve impulses carried by spinal nerves provide neural coordination, not chemical coordination.\nPancreatic digestive enzymes act in digestion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The number of digits on the forelimb and the hind limb of a frog, respectively, are:","image":"","options":["5 and 4","4 and 4","4 and 5","5 and 5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a frog, the forelimbs end in four digits.\nThe hind limbs end in five digits. They are also larger and more muscular than the forelimbs.\nOption (1) is the tempting reversal of these numbers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT among the endocrine glands of frog listed in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Thymus","Pineal body","Gonads","Fat bodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The endocrine glands of frog listed in NCERT are the pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic islets, adrenals and gonads.\nFat bodies appear in the anatomy figures near the kidneys and gonads, but they are not endocrine glands.\nThe thymus, pineal body and gonads are all in the list.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the body colour of a common Indian frog?","image":"","options":["Dorsal side uniformly pale yellow; ventral side olive green with dark spots","Both dorsal and ventral sides olive green with dark irregular spots","Dorsal side olive green with dark irregular spots; ventral side uniformly pale yellow","Dorsal side dark brown without spots; ventral side white"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The dorsal side of the frog is generally olive green with dark irregular spots.\nThe ventral side is uniformly pale yellow.\nOption (1) reverses the two surfaces.\nOption (2) wrongly gives both surfaces the same colour.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about frogs are correct?\n(i) They eat insects and thereby help protect crops.\n(ii) They serve as an important link in food chains and food webs.\n(iii) In some countries their muscular legs are eaten by humans.\n(iv) They drink water through the mouth to keep the skin moist.\n(v) Their body temperature stays constant regardless of the environment.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: frogs eat insects and protect crops.\n(ii) Correct: frogs maintain ecological balance as a link in food chains and food webs.\n(iii) Correct: frog legs are used as food in some countries.\n(iv) Incorrect: a frog never drinks water. It absorbs water through its skin.\n(v) Incorrect: frogs are poikilotherms.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (external structure of frog) with List-II (function/feature):\nList-I\n(a) Tympanum\n(b) Webbed digits of feet\n(c) Vocal sacs\n(d) Nictitating membrane\nList-II\n(i) Help in swimming\n(ii) Protects eyes in water\n(iii) Receives sound signals\n(iv) Sound production in males\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Tympanum: acts as the ear and receives sound signals, so (iii).\n(b) Webbed digits of the feet: help in swimming, so (i).\n(c) Vocal sacs: sound-producing structures found only in males, so (iv).\n(d) Nictitating membrane: protects the eyes while in water, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"A student examining two frogs wants to identify the male. Which pair of features should he look for?","image":"","options":["Webbed hind feet and a nictitating membrane","Vocal sacs and a copulatory pad on the first digit of the forelimb","A bilobed tongue and a membranous tympanum","Vocal sacs and a copulatory pad on the first digit of the hind limb"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Frogs show sexual dimorphism.\nMales have sound-producing vocal sacs and a copulatory pad on the first digit of the forelimb. Females lack both.\nWebbed feet, the nictitating membrane, the bilobed tongue and the tympanum occur in both sexes.\nOption (4) is wrong because the copulatory pad is on the forelimb, not the hind limb.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about respiration in the frog are correct?\n(i) In water, the skin acts as the respiratory organ.\n(ii) On land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs act as respiratory organs.\n(iii) During hibernation, gaseous exchange takes place mainly through the lungs.\n(iv) The lungs are a pair of elongated, pink, sac-like structures in the thorax.\n(v) Air enters the lungs through the nostrils without passing through the buccal cavity.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in water, the frog shows cutaneous respiration through the skin.\n(ii) Correct: on land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs are all respiratory organs.\n(iii) Incorrect: during aestivation and hibernation, gaseous exchange takes place through the skin.\n(iv) Correct: the lungs are elongated, pink sacs in the upper trunk (thorax).\n(v) Incorrect: air passes from the nostrils into the buccal cavity and then to the lungs.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many vasa efferentia arise from each testis of a frog?","image":"","options":["2 to 4","5 to 6","10 to 12","20 to 25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the vasa efferentia arising from the testes of a frog are 10-12 in number.\nThese ductules enter the kidney on its side and open into Bidder's canal.\nThe other ranges do not match the NCERT figure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A frog can perceive sound even though it lacks an external ear.\nReason (R): A membranous tympanum present on either side of the eyes receives sound signals, and the frog has internal ears.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: frogs have no external ear, yet they can hear.\nR is true: the tympanum receives sound signals, and the internal ear is a well-organised organ of hearing.\nBecause the tympanum and internal ear together act as the organ of hearing, R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"During peak summer, frogs shelter in deep burrows to escape extreme heat. This 'summer sleep' is called:","image":"","options":["Aestivation","Hibernation","Metamorphosis","Mimicry"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls summer sleep in burrows to avoid extreme heat aestivation.\nHibernation is winter sleep, which protects the frog from extreme cold. This is the most tempting wrong option.\nMetamorphosis is the change from tadpole to adult.\nMimicry is the term NCERT uses for protective coloration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following and choose the correct option\nColumn I: A. Touch; B. Smell; C. Cranial nerves; D. Medulla oblongata\nColumn II: i. Nasal epithelium; ii. Foramen magnum; iii. Sensory papillae; iv. Peripheral nervous system","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A. Touch → iii: In the frog, touch is sensed by the sensory papillae in the skin.\nB. Smell → i: Smell is detected by the nasal epithelium.\nC. Cranial nerves → iv: The frog's 10 pairs of cranial nerves come from the brain and belong to the peripheral nervous system.\nD. Medulla oblongata → ii: The medulla passes out through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord.\nOption (1) is tempting because it matches A and B correctly, but it swaps C and D.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In a dissected frog, the urinary bladder is best described as a:","image":"","options":["thick-walled sac lying dorsal to the rectum and opening into the cloaca","thin-walled sac lying ventral to the rectum and opening into the cloaca","thin-walled sac lying dorsal to the kidneys and opening directly into the ureters","thick-walled sac lying ventral to the stomach and opening into the duodenum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the frog's urinary bladder as thin-walled.\nIt lies ventral to the rectum and opens into the cloaca.\nOption (1) wrongly calls it thick-walled and places it dorsal to the rectum.\nOptions (3) and (4) give wrong positions and wrong openings. The bladder opens into neither the ureters nor the duodenum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In male frogs, the ureters are called urinogenital ducts.\nReason (R): In male frogs, the vasa efferentia from the testes enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal, which communicates with the duct coming out of the kidney. This duct therefore carries both urine and sperms to the cloaca.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In male frogs, the two ureters that emerge from the kidneys act as urinogenital ducts and open into the cloaca.\nR is true. The 10-12 vasa efferentia pass into the kidneys, open into Bidder's canal, and connect with this duct.\nBecause the same duct carries both urine and sperms, it is called urinogenital. So R correctly explains A.\nThis differs from females, where the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In male frogs, the ureters act as urinogenital ducts, and sperms reach them after passing through the vasa efferentia and Bidder's canal inside the kidneys.\nStatement II: In female frogs, the ovaries are functionally connected with the kidneys, and the oviducts join the ureters to open into the cloaca by a common duct.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The vasa efferentia arise from the testes and enter the kidneys, where they open into Bidder's canal. Bidder's canal communicates with the urinogenital duct (ureter), which opens into the cloaca.\nStatement II is incorrect on both points. The ovaries lie near the kidneys but have no functional connection with them. The oviducts and ureters also open separately into the cloaca.\nSo the urinogenital link exists only in males.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In a frog, the triangular structure called sinus venosus joins which chamber of the heart?","image":"","options":["Left atrium","Ventricle","Conus arteriosus","Right atrium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The sinus venosus is a triangular structure that joins the right atrium.\nIt receives blood from the body through the major veins called vena cava.\nThe left atrium is a common wrong choice. The sinus venosus is associated with the right side of the heart.\nThe conus arteriosus is connected to the ventricle, not to the sinus venosus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Cutaneous respiration\nB. Pulmonary respiration\nC. Lungs of frog\nD. Source of oxygen for the skin under water\nList-II\n(i) Paired, elongated, pink sacs in the thorax\n(ii) Gas exchange across the skin by diffusion\n(iii) Dissolved oxygen in water\n(iv) Respiration by lungs\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Cutaneous respiration is gas exchange through the skin by diffusion.\nB-(iv): Pulmonary respiration is respiration by lungs.\nC-(i): The lungs are paired, elongated, pink, sac-like structures in the thorax.\nD-(iii): Under water, the skin exchanges the oxygen dissolved in the water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In a male frog, the testes are attached to the upper part of the kidneys by a double fold of peritoneum. What is this fold called?","image":"","options":["Mesorchium","Mesovarium","Pericardium","Mesentery"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: the pair of yellowish ovoid testes is attached to the upper part of the kidneys by a double fold of peritoneum called the mesorchium.\nMesovarium is the term for the fold that holds the ovary, not the testis.\nPericardium is the membrane that covers the heart.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Aestivation\nB. Hibernation\nC. Poikilothermy\nD. Mimicry\nList-II\n(i) Body temperature varies with the environment\n(ii) Protective coloration to hide from enemies\n(iii) Winter sleep in deep burrows\n(iv) Summer sleep in deep burrows\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iv): Aestivation is summer sleep, which protects the frog from extreme heat.\nB-(iii): Hibernation is winter sleep, which protects the frog from extreme cold.\nC-(i): Poikilotherms (cold-blooded animals) have a body temperature that changes with the surroundings.\nD-(ii): NCERT calls the colour change used for camouflage 'mimicry', a form of protective coloration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with reference to the circulatory system of frog.\nList-I\n(A) Sinus venosus\n(B) Conus arteriosus\n(C) Pericardium\n(D) Hepatic portal system\nList-II\n(i) Membrane covering the heart\n(ii) Venous connection between intestine and liver\n(iii) Receives blood through venae cavae\n(iv) Sac into which the ventricle opens on the ventral side\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): The sinus venosus receives blood through the venae cavae and joins the right atrium.\nB-(iv): The ventricle opens into the sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart.\nC-(i): The pericardium is the membrane that covers the heart.\nD-(ii): The hepatic portal system is the special venous connection between the liver and the intestine.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Respiration in a frog carried out by means of its lungs is termed","image":"","options":["Cutaneous respiration","Branchial respiration","Pulmonary respiration","Anaerobic respiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT names respiration by lungs pulmonary respiration.\nCutaneous respiration is gas exchange through the skin, so (1) is wrong.\nBranchial respiration is breathing by gills, so (2) is wrong.\nAnaerobic respiration is a cellular process that does not use oxygen, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In frogs, lymph is different from blood.\nReason (R): Lymph lacks a few proteins and RBCs that are present in blood.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT clearly states that lymph is different from blood.\nR is true. Lymph lacks a few proteins and RBCs.\nThe absence of these components is exactly what makes lymph different from blood, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"An adult frog is sitting on land. Which of the following does NOT act as a respiratory surface for it?","image":"","options":["Skin","Buccal cavity","Lungs","Gills"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"On land, NCERT lists the buccal cavity, skin and lungs as the respiratory organs of the frog.\nRespiration by lungs is called pulmonary respiration. In water, the skin alone is used (cutaneous respiration).\nGills are not listed as a respiratory organ of the adult frog in either habitat.\nSkin is a tempting choice, but it works both on land and in water.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the sense organs of frog are correct?\n(a) The eyes are simple eyes, possessing only one unit.\n(b) An external ear is absent, and only the tympanum is visible externally.\n(c) The ear functions in both hearing and balancing.\n(d) The nasal epithelium is a well-organised sense organ like the eye.\n(e) Sensory papillae are organs of touch.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the frog's eyes are simple, with one unit each.\n(b) Correct: the external ear is absent, and only the tympanum is seen.\n(c) Correct: the ear serves both hearing and equilibrium.\n(d) Incorrect: only the eyes and internal ears are well-organised. The nasal epithelium is a cellular aggregation around nerve endings.\n(e) Correct: sensory papillae are organs of touch.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the external morphology of a frog is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The body is divisible into head and trunk, as a neck and a tail are absent","The hind limbs are larger and more muscular than the forelimbs","The nictitating membrane protects the eyes mainly while the frog is on dry land","The copulatory pad of a male frog is found on the first digit of the forelimb"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the adult frog has only a head and a trunk. It has no neck or tail.\n(2) Correct: the hind limbs are larger and more muscular than the forelimbs and have five webbed digits.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT says the nictitating membrane protects the eyes while the frog is in water, not on land.\n(4) Correct: the copulatory pad is on the first digit of the forelimb, not the hind limb.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of the male frog's urinogenital system shows yellowish ovoid testes attached to the kidneys, with 10-12 fine ducts arising from them. Which sequence correctly traces the path of sperms to the exterior?","image":"","options":["Testis → Bidder's canal → vasa efferentia → urinogenital duct → cloaca","Testis → vasa efferentia → urinogenital duct → Bidder's canal → cloaca","Testis → vasa efferentia → Bidder's canal → urinogenital duct → cloaca","Testis → vasa efferentia → urinary bladder → urinogenital duct → cloaca"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The 10-12 fine ducts arising from the testes are the vasa efferentia.\nThey enter the kidneys on their side and open into Bidder's canal.\nBidder's canal communicates with the urinogenital duct, which leaves the kidney and opens into the cloaca.\nThe urinary bladder opens into the cloaca but is not on the sperm pathway.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Tissue\n(B) Organ\n(C) Organ system\n(D) Division of labour\nList-II\n(I) Stomach\n(II) Digestive system\n(III) Similar cells with intercellular substances performing a specific function\n(IV) Work split among cells, tissues, organs and organ systems for survival of the body\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-III: a tissue is a group of similar cells with intercellular substances performing a specific function.\nB-I: the stomach is an organ formed from tissues.\nC-II: the digestive system is an organ system.\nD-IV: division of labour means cells, tissues, organs and organ systems split up the work so that the whole body survives.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The eyes of a frog are best described as:","image":"","options":["Compound eyes made of many units","Simple eyes possessing only one unit","A single median eye on the head","Eyes reduced to light-sensitive spots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A frog has a pair of spherical eyes lodged in the orbits of the skull.\nNCERT calls them simple eyes because each has only one unit.\nCompound eyes made of many units occur in insects, not frogs.\nFrog eyes are paired, bulged and well organised, not a single median eye or mere eye spots.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which nitrogenous waste does an adult frog mainly excrete, and what is it called on that basis?","image":"","options":["Ammonia; ammonotelic","Urea; ureotelic","Uric acid; uricotelic","Urea; uricotelic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the frog excretes urea, so it is a ureotelic animal.\nAmmonotelic animals excrete ammonia, and uricotelic animals excrete uric acid, so options (1) and (3) name the wrong waste.\nOption (4) names the right waste but the wrong term. 'Uricotelic' refers to uric acid excretion, not urea.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The heart of a frog consists of how many chambers?","image":"","options":["Two: one atrium and one ventricle","Three: two atria and one ventricle","Three: one atrium and two ventricles","Four: two atria and two ventricles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the frog heart is a muscular, three-chambered structure.\nIt has two atria and a single ventricle.\nOption (3) is tempting because it also says three chambers, but it reverses the arrangement. The frog has only one ventricle.\nA four-chambered heart is not found in frogs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for the frog.\nList-I:\nA. Erythrocytes\nB. Leucocytes\nC. Plasma\nD. Lymph nodes\nList-II:\nI. Part of the lymphatic system along with lymph channels\nII. Fluid part of blood in which the cells are present\nIII. White blood cells\nIV. Nucleated cells containing haemoglobin\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-IV: Erythrocytes (RBCs) of frog are nucleated and contain haemoglobin.\nB-III: Leucocytes are the white blood cells.\nC-II: Plasma is the fluid part of blood. Blood consists of plasma and cells.\nD-I: Lymph nodes form the lymphatic system together with lymph and lymph channels.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following external features would be seen ONLY in a male frog and NOT in a female frog?\n(i) Sound-producing vocal sacs\n(ii) Membranous tympanum on either side of the eyes\n(iii) Copulatory pad on the first digit of the forelimb\n(iv) Webbed digits on the hind limbs\n(v) Nictitating membrane over the eyes","image":"","options":["One","Three","Four","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Male only: vocal sacs are absent in females.\n(ii) Both sexes: the tympanum is present in all frogs.\n(iii) Male only: the copulatory pad on the first forelimb digit is absent in females.\n(iv) Both sexes: webbed hind digits help all frogs swim.\n(v) Both sexes: all frogs have a nictitating membrane.\nSo two features, (i) and (iii), show sexual dimorphism.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the heart of a frog is incorrect?","image":"","options":["It has three chambers: two atria and one ventricle","It is covered by a membrane called pericardium","A triangular sinus venosus joins the left atrium and receives blood from the pulmonary veins","The ventricle opens into a sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the frog's heart has two atria and one ventricle.\n(2) Correct: the heart is enclosed by the pericardium.\n(3) Incorrect: the sinus venosus joins the right atrium, not the left. It receives blood through the major veins called vena cava.\n(4) Correct: the ventricle opens into the conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the blood and lymph of frog is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Frog has a lymphatic system in addition to the blood vascular system, and lymph flows through lymph channels","Because lymph lacks RBCs, it cannot carry oxygen bound to haemoglobin","Blood delivers nutrients, gases and water to the respective sites, and its flow is maintained by the pumping heart","Like mammalian RBCs, frog RBCs lack a nucleus, which leaves more space for haemoglobin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT says frogs have a lymphatic system made of lymph, lymph channels and lymph nodes.\n(2) Correct: haemoglobin is present only in RBCs, and lymph lacks RBCs.\n(3) Correct: NCERT says blood carries nutrients, gases and water, and the muscular heart pumps it.\n(4) Incorrect: frog RBCs are nucleated, unlike mammalian RBCs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In female frogs, the ureters act as urinogenital ducts.\nReason (R): In female frogs, the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false: the ureters act as urinogenital ducts only in male frogs. There, sperms reach the ureter through the vasa efferentia and Bidder's canal.\nR is true: in females, the ovaries have no functional connection with the kidneys. The ureters carry only urine, and the oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A tissue is a group of similar cells along with intercellular substances that performs a specific function.\n(ii) In plants and microbes, the term morphology means only the study of externally visible form.\n(iii) In animals, the term anatomy is conventionally used for the study of the morphology of internal organs.\n(iv) Epithelia have two free surfaces, each facing a body fluid or the outside environment.\n(v) Two or more organs that perform a common function through physical and/or chemical interaction form an organ system.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of a tissue.\n(ii) Correct: for plants and microbes, morphology precisely means the study of external form only.\n(iii) Correct: anatomy conventionally means the study of the morphology of internal organs in animals.\n(iv) Incorrect: epithelia have only ONE free surface, which faces a body fluid or the outside environment. Their cells are connected at junctions.\n(v) Correct: this is how NCERT defines an organ system, for example the digestive system.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the sense organs and nervous system of frog are correct?\n(i) Eyes are simple, possessing only one unit.\n(ii) The external ear is absent; only the tympanum is visible externally.\n(iii) Sensory papillae and taste buds are well-organised structures like the eyes.\n(iv) The ear functions in both hearing and balancing.\n(v) The medulla oblongata passes out through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a frog's eyes are simple eyes with a single unit.\n(ii) Correct: the external ear is absent, and only the tympanum can be seen.\n(iii) Incorrect: only the eyes and internal ears are well organised. Sensory papillae and taste buds are cellular aggregations around nerve endings.\n(iv) Correct: the ear serves hearing and equilibrium.\n(v) Correct: this is how NCERT describes the medulla oblongata.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In frog, venous blood from the lower parts of the body passes to the kidneys through a special venous connection before it returns to the heart.\nStatement II: In frog, blood from the intestine reaches the liver through the renal portal system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT mentions two special venous connections in frog.\nThe connection between the kidney and the lower parts of the body is the renal portal system. So blood from the lower body passes through the kidneys first. Statement I is correct.\nThe connection between the intestine and the liver is the hepatic portal system, not the renal portal system. Statement II mislabels it and is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with respect to the frog.\nList-I:\nA. Olfactory lobes\nB. Optic lobes\nC. Cerebellum\nD. Cranium\nList-II:\nI. Part of hind-brain\nII. Part of fore-brain\nIII. Bony box enclosing the brain\nIV. Characteristic structures of mid-brain\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-II: The fore-brain includes the olfactory lobes, the paired cerebral hemispheres and the unpaired diencephalon.\nB-IV: The mid-brain is characterised by a pair of optic lobes.\nC-I: The hind-brain consists of the cerebellum and the medulla oblongata.\nD-III: The cranium is the bony brain box that encloses the brain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as prominent endocrine glands of the frog?\nPituitary, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic islets, gonads, gall bladder, liver, adrenals","image":"","options":["Four","Five","Six","Seven"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Endocrine glands of frog: pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic islets, adrenals and gonads.\nFrom the given list, six are endocrine: pituitary, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic islets, gonads and adrenals.\nThe liver secretes bile, so it is a digestive gland, not an endocrine gland.\nThe gall bladder only stores bile and secretes no hormone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The blood vascular system of frog is of the closed type.\nReason (R): The heart of frog has three chambers: two atria and one ventricle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT describes the vascular system of frog as well-developed and of the closed type.\nR is true. The frog heart has two atria and one ventricle.\nA closed system means that blood flows within vessels. This does not depend on the number of heart chambers, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pairs of sense organs and their features in the frog are correctly matched?\n(i) Sensory papillae – touch\n(ii) Taste buds – taste\n(iii) Nasal epithelium – vision\n(iv) Eyes – compound eyes made of many units\n(v) Ear – hearing as well as balancing\n(vi) External ear – visible as a distinct flap","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: sensory papillae are organs of touch.\n(ii) Correct: taste buds are organs of taste.\n(iii) Incorrect: the nasal epithelium is for smell. The eyes are for vision.\n(iv) Incorrect: the frog's eyes are simple eyes with only one unit.\n(v) Correct: the ear serves both hearing and equilibrium.\n(vi) Incorrect: the external ear is absent. Only the tympanum is visible externally.\nThree pairs are correctly matched.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Frogs are not seen during peak summer and peak winter.\nReason (R): Frogs excrete urea as their main nitrogenous waste.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In peak summer and winter, frogs shelter in deep burrows. These periods are called aestivation and hibernation.\nR is true. The frog excretes urea, so it is ureotelic.\nR does not explain A. The burrowing protects the frog from extreme heat and cold and has nothing to do with the type of nitrogenous waste.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for structures of the frog.\nList-I:\nA. Mesorchium\nB. Nictitating membrane\nC. Tympanum\nD. Pericardium\nList-II:\n(i) Membrane covering the heart\n(ii) Receives sound signals\n(iii) Protects the eyes while in water\n(iv) Double fold of peritoneum attaching the testes to the kidneys\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(iv): The mesorchium is a double fold of peritoneum that holds the testes to the upper part of the kidneys.\nB-(iii): The nictitating membrane covers the bulged eyes and protects them in water.\nC-(ii): The membranous tympanum on either side of the eyes receives sound.\nD-(i): The pericardium covers the heart.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In male frogs, the ureters act as urinogenital ducts that open into the cloaca.\nStatement II: In female frogs, the ovaries are functionally connected to the kidneys, and ova pass through the ureters.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In males, the ureters carry both urine and sperms, so they serve as urinogenital ducts that open into the cloaca.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says the ovaries lie near the kidneys but have no functional connection with them. Ova travel through separate oviducts that open into the cloaca.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The body temperature of a frog rises and falls with the temperature of its surroundings.\nStatement II: Because of this, frogs are classified as homeotherms (warm blooded).\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A frog's body temperature varies with its environment.\nStatement II is incorrect. Animals whose body temperature varies with the environment are called cold blooded, or poikilotherms.\nHomeotherms keep a constant body temperature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for the frog's circulatory system.\nList-I\n(A) Sinus venosus\n(B) Conus arteriosus\n(C) Hepatic portal system\n(D) Renal portal system\nList-II\n(i) Special venous connection between kidney and lower parts of the body\n(ii) Receives blood through vena cava\n(iii) Special venous connection between liver and intestine\n(iv) Sac into which the ventricle opens","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) The sinus venosus receives blood through the vena cava, so A matches (ii).\n(B) The conus arteriosus is the ventral sac into which the ventricle opens, so B matches (iv).\n(C) The hepatic portal system links the liver and the intestine, so C matches (iii).\n(D) The renal portal system links the kidney and the lower parts of the body, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the frog, partially digested food leaving the stomach is called ______ and it first enters the ______.","image":"","options":["Chyle; rectum","Bolus; oesophagus","Chyme; cloaca","Chyme; duodenum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"HCl and gastric juices act on food in the stomach and partially digest it. This partially digested food is called chyme.\nChyme passes into the duodenum, which is the first part of the small intestine.\nThe cloaca is the terminal chamber, not the next part after the stomach.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the eyes of a frog is correct?","image":"","options":["They are flat and lie on the surface of the skull without any orbit","They are bulged and covered by a nictitating membrane that protects them in water","They are compound eyes, each made of many units","They are cellular aggregations around nerve endings, like taste buds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option 2 is correct: frog eyes are bulged and covered by a nictitating membrane that protects them in water.\nOption 1 is wrong: the eyes are spherical and sit in the orbits of the skull.\nOption 3 is wrong: they are simple eyes with only one unit each.\nOption 4 is wrong: eyes are well-organised sense organs, not cellular aggregations.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In plants and microbes, the term morphology precisely means only the study of form or externally visible features.\nStatement II: In animals, the word anatomy is conventionally used for the study of morphology of internal organs.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT says that for plants and microbes, morphology precisely means only the study of external form.\nStatement II is correct: in animals, anatomy conventionally refers to the morphology of internal organs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In frog, the ear is an organ of hearing as well as of balancing (equilibrium).\nStatement II: In frog, a pinna is present externally to collect sound waves.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that the frog's ear serves both hearing and equilibrium.\nStatement II is incorrect: frogs have no external ear, so there is no pinna.\nOnly the tympanum is visible from outside, and it receives sound signals.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"During peak summer, frogs disappear from view and remain in deep burrows to protect themselves from extreme heat. What is this phenomenon called?","image":"","options":["Aestivation","Hibernation","Metamorphosis","Camouflage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: frogs are not seen during peak summer and winter because they shelter in deep burrows.\nThe summer sleep, which protects them from extreme heat, is called aestivation.\nHibernation is the tempting wrong option, but it is the winter sleep, which protects them from extreme cold.\nMetamorphosis is the change from tadpole to adult. Camouflage is colour change used for hiding from enemies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Frogs are described as poikilotherms because they:","image":"","options":["maintain a constant body temperature in all seasons","can change body colour to match their surroundings","respire through skin, lungs and buccal cavity","have a body temperature that varies with the temperature of the environment"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A poikilotherm, or cold-blooded animal, has no constant body temperature. Its body temperature changes with the surroundings.\nFrogs fit this description.\nA constant body temperature describes homeotherms, so option (1) is wrong.\nColour change is camouflage, not poikilothermy.\nThe use of several respiratory surfaces is unrelated to body temperature.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"To which one of the following categories does adipose tissue belong?","image":"","options":["Epithelial","Connective","Muscular","Neural"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: adipose tissue is a type of loose connective tissue found mainly beneath the skin.\nWhy it fits: its cells are specialised to store fats. Excess nutrients are converted into fat and stored here.\nWhy not epithelial: epithelium covers or lines surfaces. Adipose tissue instead has cells scattered with matrix, which is the defining feature of connective tissue.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the frog's digestive system are correct?\n(i) The oesophagus is a long, coiled tube.\n(ii) Digestion in the stomach occurs by the action of HCl and gastric juices.\n(iii) The gall bladder secretes bile.\n(iv) Digested food is absorbed by finger-like folds called villi and microvilli in the inner wall of the intestine.\n(v) Undigested solid waste moves into the rectum and passes out through the cloaca.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the oesophagus is a short tube.\n(ii) Correct: HCl and gastric juices from the stomach walls digest food.\n(iii) Incorrect: the liver secretes bile; the gall bladder only stores it.\n(iv) Correct: villi and microvilli absorb digested food.\n(v) Correct: waste passes from the rectum out through the cloaca.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about frogs are correct?\n(i) Frogs belong to class Amphibia of phylum Chordata.\n(ii) Rana tigrina is the most common species of frog found in India.\n(iii) Frogs maintain a constant body temperature and are therefore homeotherms.\n(iv) The ability of frogs to change colour to hide from enemies is a protective coloration called mimicry.\n(v) Frogs benefit mankind by eating insects and thus protecting crops.\n(vi) Frogs are a link in food chains but not in food webs.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: frogs live on land and in freshwater and are amphibians of phylum Chordata.\n(ii) Correct: Rana tigrina is the common Indian frog.\n(iii) Incorrect: their body temperature varies with the environment, so they are cold blooded (poikilotherms).\n(iv) Correct: NCERT calls this camouflage-type protective coloration mimicry.\n(v) Correct: frogs eat insects and so protect crops.\n(vi) Incorrect: frogs are an important link in both food chains and food webs.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following with reference to cockroach and choose the correct option\nColumn I: A. Phallomere; B. Gonopore; C. Spermatophore; D. Ovarioles\nColumn II: i. Chain of developing ova; ii. Bundles of sperm; iii. Opening of the ejaculatory duct; iv. The external genitalia","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Phallomere - iv: phallomeres are chitinous structures forming the male external genitalia.\nB. Gonopore - iii: it is the opening of the ejaculatory duct.\nC. Spermatophore - ii: sperms are stored and transferred as bundles called spermatophores.\nD. Ovarioles - i: each ovariole contains a chain of developing ova.\nThis gives A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i, which is option (2).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about respiration in frog:\n(i) In water, oxygen dissolved in the water is exchanged through the skin by diffusion.\n(ii) On land, the lungs are the only respiratory organs.\n(iii) The lungs are a pair of elongated, pink, sac-like structures present in the upper part of the trunk (thorax).\n(iv) Air passes from the nostrils directly into the lungs without entering the buccal cavity.\n(v) Respiration through the lungs is called pulmonary respiration.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in water, the skin is the respiratory organ (cutaneous respiration), and dissolved oxygen is exchanged by diffusion.\n(ii) Incorrect: on land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs all act as respiratory organs.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT describes the lungs as paired, elongated, pink, sac-like structures in the thorax.\n(iv) Incorrect: air enters through the nostrils into the buccal cavity and then passes to the lungs.\n(v) Correct: respiration by lungs is called pulmonary respiration.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure in the male frog) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\nA. Vasa efferentia\nB. Bidder's canal\nC. Urinogenital duct\nD. Mesorchium\nList-II\n(i) Lies within the kidney and receives the vasa efferentia\n(ii) Double fold of peritoneum holding the testes\n(iii) 10 to 12 in number and arise from the testes\n(iv) Comes out of the kidney and opens into the cloaca\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-(iii): The vasa efferentia are 10 to 12 in number and arise from the testes.\nB-(i): The vasa efferentia enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal.\nC-(iv): The urinogenital duct comes out of the kidney and opens into the cloaca.\nD-(ii): The mesorchium is a peritoneal fold that attaches the testes to the kidneys.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Three frogs are observed: Frog P is hibernating in a deep burrow in winter, Frog Q is swimming submerged in a pond, and Frog R is sitting on wet grass. Which statement about their respiration is correct?","image":"","options":["Skin is a respiratory surface in all three frogs","Frogs P and Q both depend mainly on lungs for gaseous exchange","Frog R respires only through its lungs","Frog P respires through its buccal cavity and lungs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Frog P is hibernating, and NCERT states that gas exchange then occurs through the skin. So (4) is wrong.\nFrog Q is in water, where the skin is the respiratory organ. So (2) is wrong.\nFrog R is on land, where the buccal cavity, skin and lungs all act together. So (3) is wrong.\nThe skin is therefore a respiratory surface in all three frogs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In frog, the medulla oblongata leaves the brain box and continues as the spinal cord by passing through an opening called the:","image":"","options":["Foramen of Monro","Obturator foramen","Foramen magnum","Optic foramen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The brain of a frog is enclosed in a bony brain box called the cranium.\nThe medulla oblongata passes out of the cranium through the foramen magnum.\nIt then continues as the spinal cord, which is enclosed in the vertebral column.\nThe foramen of Monro is an opening between the ventricles inside the brain. It is not an exit from the skull, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly shows the sequence of parts through which food passes in the alimentary canal of a frog?","image":"","options":["Mouth → Pharynx → Buccal cavity → Oesophagus → Stomach → Intestine → Rectum → Cloaca","Mouth → Buccal cavity → Pharynx → Oesophagus → Stomach → Intestine → Rectum → Cloaca","Mouth → Buccal cavity → Oesophagus → Pharynx → Stomach → Rectum → Intestine → Cloaca","Mouth → Buccal cavity → Pharynx → Stomach → Oesophagus → Intestine → Cloaca → Rectum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: the mouth opens into the buccal cavity, which leads to the oesophagus through the pharynx.\nThe short oesophagus opens into the stomach. The stomach continues as the intestine and then the rectum, which opens outside through the cloaca.\nOption (1) wrongly places the pharynx before the buccal cavity.\nOptions (3) and (4) misplace the oesophagus, rectum or cloaca.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes stained smears of frog blood and human blood under a microscope. Which feature of the RBCs would help her identify the frog smear?","image":"","options":["Presence of haemoglobin in the RBCs","Presence of a nucleus in the RBCs","RBCs appearing as one type of blood cell along with WBCs","Presence of RBCs suspended in plasma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that frog RBCs are nucleated, while mature human RBCs lack a nucleus.\nHaemoglobin is present in the RBCs of both, so option (1) cannot tell them apart.\nBoth bloods have RBCs and WBCs suspended in plasma, so options (3) and (4) do not help either.\nOnly the nucleus in the RBCs distinguishes the frog smear.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following and choose the correct option\nColumn I: A. Adipose tissue; B. Stratified epithelium; C. Hyaline cartilage; D. Fluid connective tissue\nColumn II: i. Nose; ii. Blood; iii. Skin; iv. Fat storage","image":"","options":["A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Adipose tissue - iv: its cells are specialised to store fat.\nB. Stratified epithelium - iii: stratified squamous epithelium covers the dry surface of the skin.\nC. Hyaline cartilage - i: cartilage supports the tip of the nose.\nD. Fluid connective tissue - ii: blood is a fluid connective tissue.\nThis gives A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii, which is option (2).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The heart of a frog lies in the upper part of the body cavity. Which membrane covers it?","image":"","options":["Peritoneum","Mesorchium","Pericardium","Nictitating membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the frog heart is covered by a membrane called the pericardium.\nThe peritoneum lines the body cavity.\nThe mesorchium is a double fold of peritoneum that attaches the testes to the kidneys.\nThe nictitating membrane protects the eyes while the frog is in water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the sense organs of frog is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Damage to the internal ears would affect both hearing and maintenance of body equilibrium","The organs of touch and smell are well-organised structures, just like the eyes","The eyes are a pair of spherical structures situated in the orbits of the skull","Taste buds are cellular aggregations around nerve endings"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option 1 is correct: the frog's ear serves both hearing and balance.\nOption 2 is incorrect: sensory papillae (touch) and nasal epithelium (smell) are only cellular aggregations around nerve endings. Only the eyes and internal ears are well organised.\nOption 3 is correct: the paired spherical eyes lie in the orbits of the skull.\nOption 4 is correct: taste buds are cellular aggregations around nerve endings.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The skin of a frog feels smooth and slippery to touch. This is mainly due to the presence of:","image":"","options":["A thick layer of keratinised scales","Mucus on the skin surface","Oil secreted by sebaceous glands","A waxy cuticle like that of leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that frog skin is smooth and slippery because of mucus, and it is always kept moist.\nScales and keratinised layers are absent in frog skin.\nSebaceous (oil) glands are a mammalian feature.\nA waxy cuticle is found on plant surfaces, not on amphibians.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the frog, bile is secreted by the ______ and stored in the ______.","image":"","options":["Liver; gall bladder","Gall bladder; liver","Pancreas; gall bladder","Liver; urinary bladder"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The liver secretes bile, and the gall bladder stores it.\nOption (2) reverses the roles: the gall bladder only stores bile and does not make it.\nThe pancreas produces pancreatic juice, not bile.\nThe urinary bladder is part of the excretory system.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as endocrine glands of the frog?\nPituitary, Thymus, Gall bladder, Adrenals, Pineal body, Liver, Parathyroid","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the frog's endocrine glands as pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic islets, adrenals and gonads.\nFrom the given list, the endocrine glands are pituitary, thymus, adrenals, pineal body and parathyroid, which makes 5.\nThe liver is a digestive gland that secretes bile. The gall bladder only stores bile. Neither is an endocrine gland.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following types of cell is involved in making of the inner walls of blood vessels?","image":"","options":["Cuboidal epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Stratified epithelium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the inner lining of blood vessels (endothelium) is a single layer of thin, flat cells with irregular borders. This is simple squamous epithelium.\nWhy it fits: the thin lining allows easy diffusion of substances across the vessel wall.\nWhy not the others: cuboidal epithelium lines ducts of glands and kidney tubules, and columnar epithelium lines the stomach and intestine. Stratified epithelium is multilayered and protective, as in skin, so it is too thick for diffusion.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the nervous system of frog are correct?\n(a) Ten pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain.\n(b) The diencephalon is a paired structure of the fore-brain.\n(c) The mid-brain is characterised by a pair of optic lobes.\n(d) The cerebellum is a part of the fore-brain.\n(e) The medulla oblongata passes out through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord.\n(f) The fore-brain includes olfactory lobes and paired cerebral hemispheres.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the frog has ten pairs of cranial nerves.\n(b) Incorrect: the diencephalon is unpaired.\n(c) Correct: the mid-brain bears a pair of optic lobes.\n(d) Incorrect: the cerebellum belongs to the hind-brain, along with the medulla oblongata.\n(e) Correct: the medulla oblongata exits through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord.\n(f) Correct: the fore-brain has olfactory lobes and paired cerebral hemispheres.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Two or more organs together form an organ system when they perform a common function through physical and/or chemical interaction.\nReason (R): The complexity seen in organs and organ systems shows a discernable trend known as the evolutionary trend.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT defines an organ system as two or more organs performing a common function by physical and/or chemical interaction.\nR is true: the complexity in organs and organ systems displays a discernable trend called the evolutionary trend.\nR describes how complexity changes over evolution. It does not explain what makes a group of organs an organ system, so R is not the explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The frog excretes its nitrogenous waste mainly as urea. On this basis, the frog is described as","image":"","options":["ammonotelic","uricotelic","ureotelic","aminotelic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the frog excretes urea, so it is a ureotelic animal.\nAmmonotelic animals excrete mainly ammonia, and uricotelic animals excrete mainly uric acid.\n'Aminotelic' is not the term NCERT uses for frogs.\nA common mistake is to pick ammonotelic because frogs live in water. NCERT clearly classes the adult frog as ureotelic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the frog, bile emulsifies fats.\nStatement II: In the frog, final digestion of food takes place in the stomach.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that bile emulsifies fat.\nStatement II is incorrect: the stomach only partially digests food into chyme. Final digestion takes place in the intestine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The bulged eyes of a frog are covered by a nictitating membrane. What is the main role of this membrane?","image":"","options":["Receiving sound signals from the surroundings","Producing sound during the breeding season","Protecting the eyes while the frog is in water","Absorbing water for the body"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the nictitating membrane covers the bulged eyes and protects them while the frog is in water.\nSound is received by the tympanum, which lies on either side of the eyes.\nSound is produced with the help of vocal sacs, found only in males.\nWater is absorbed through the skin, not the eyes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Frogs show sexual dimorphism. Which of the following structures would help you identify a male frog?","image":"","options":["Nictitating membrane over the eyes","Membranous tympanum on either side of the eyes","Sound-producing vocal sacs","Webbed digits on the hind limbs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Male frogs have sound-producing vocal sacs. They also have a copulatory pad on the first digit of the fore limbs. Both are absent in females.\nThe nictitating membrane, tympanum and webbed hind feet are found in both sexes, so they cannot be used to tell males from females.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following shows the correct route of sperms from the site of production to the exterior in a male frog?","image":"","options":["Testis → Vasa efferentia → Bidder's canal → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca","Testis → Bidder's canal → Vasa efferentia → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca","Testis → Vasa efferentia → Urinogenital duct → Bidder's canal → Cloaca","Testis → Vasa efferentia → Oviduct → Cloaca"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Sperms leave the testis through 10-12 vasa efferentia.\nStep 2: The vasa efferentia enter the kidney and open into Bidder's canal.\nStep 3: Bidder's canal communicates with the urinogenital duct coming out of the kidney.\nStep 4: The urinogenital duct opens into the cloaca.\nOption (2) reverses the first two structures, and option (4) wrongly includes the oviduct, which is a female duct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In male frogs, the ureters act as urinogenital ducts that open into the cloaca.\nStatement II: In female frogs, the oviducts join the ureters before opening into the cloaca.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in males, the ureters carry both urine and sperms, so they act as urinogenital ducts that open into the cloaca.\nStatement II is incorrect: in females, the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\nIn addition, the ovaries have no functional connection with the kidneys.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A frog can continue gaseous exchange while it stays in a deep burrow during peak summer.\nReason (R): During aestivation, gaseous exchange in the frog takes place through its skin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. During summer sleep (aestivation) the frog shelters in a deep burrow and stays alive, so it must keep exchanging gases.\nR is true. NCERT states that during aestivation and hibernation, gaseous exchange takes place through the skin.\nR explains A, because the skin is the surface that allows respiration inside the burrow.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the heart of a frog, the triangular structure that receives blood through the venae cavae joins which chamber?","image":"","options":["Right atrium","Left atrium","Ventricle","Conus arteriosus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The triangular structure attached to the frog heart is the sinus venosus.\nIt receives blood from the major veins, the venae cavae.\nNCERT states that the sinus venosus joins the right atrium.\nThe conus arteriosus is a tempting choice, but it is the sac into which the ventricle opens. It does not receive venous blood.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is true for cockroach?","image":"","options":["The number of ovarioles in each ovary are ten.","The larval stage is called caterpillar","Anal styles are absent in females","They are ureotelic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) False: each ovary of a cockroach has eight ovarioles, not ten.\n(2) False: cockroach shows paurometabolous development. The young stage is a nymph; a caterpillar is the larva of butterflies and moths.\n(3) True: a pair of anal styles is present only in males, so females lack them.\n(4) False: cockroaches excrete uric acid, so they are uricotelic, not ureotelic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is not a connective tissue?","image":"","options":["Bone","Cartilage","Blood","Muscles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT groups connective tissue into loose, dense, specialised and fluid types.\n(1) Bone: specialised connective tissue.\n(2) Cartilage: specialised connective tissue.\n(3) Blood: fluid connective tissue.\n(4) Muscle: a separate basic tissue type (muscular tissue) made of contractile fibres. It is not a connective tissue.\nBlood is the tempting distractor, but it is a connective tissue because it has cells in a fluid matrix (plasma).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the blood of a frog is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Blood is composed of plasma and cells.","The blood cells include erythrocytes, leucocytes and platelets.","The RBCs are non-nucleated and contain haemoglobin.","Blood carries nutrients, gases and water to the respective sites during circulation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: frog blood consists of plasma and cells.\n(2) Correct: the blood cells are RBCs (erythrocytes), WBCs (leucocytes) and platelets.\n(3) Incorrect: frog RBCs are nucleated. They do contain the red pigment haemoglobin, but the 'non-nucleated' part is wrong.\n(4) Correct: blood carries nutrients, gases and water during circulation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the external morphology of a frog is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A pair of nostrils is present above the mouth","The body is divisible into head, neck and trunk","A membranous tympanum is present on either side of the eyes","The skin is always maintained in a moist condition"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: a pair of nostrils lies above the mouth.\n(2) Incorrect: the frog body is divisible only into head and trunk, and both a neck and a tail are absent.\n(3) Correct: a tympanum on each side of the eyes receives sound signals.\n(4) Correct: the skin is kept moist and slippery by mucus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following sense organs of frog are merely cellular aggregations around nerve endings, rather than well-organised structures?\nSensory papillae, taste buds, nasal epithelium, eyes, internal ears","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says the eyes and internal ears are well-organised structures.\nThe remaining sense organs are cellular aggregations around nerve endings.\nThese are sensory papillae, taste buds and nasal epithelium.\nThat makes 3 such organs in the list.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about reproduction and development in frogs are correct?\n(i) Fertilisation is internal and takes place inside the oviduct.\n(ii) The testes are yellowish and ovoid.\n(iii) Development includes a tadpole larva that undergoes metamorphosis to form the adult.\n(iv) The cloaca is a small median chamber through which faecal matter, urine and sperms pass to the exterior.\n(v) The oviducts open into the urinary bladder.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: fertilisation in frogs is external and takes place in water.\n(ii) Correct: the testes are a pair of yellowish ovoid bodies.\n(iii) Correct: the tadpole larva undergoes metamorphosis to become the adult.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT describes the cloaca as a small median chamber that passes faeces, urine and sperms.\n(v) Incorrect: the oviducts open separately into the cloaca, not into the bladder.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The bulged eyes of a frog are covered by a nictitating membrane. Its main role is to:","image":"","options":["Receive sound signals from the surroundings","Protect the eyes while the frog is in water","Absorb dissolved oxygen for respiration","Help the frog change colour for camouflage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the nictitating membrane as a covering over the bulged eyes that protects them while the frog is in water.\nReceiving sound signals is the job of the tympanum, which lies on either side of the eyes.\nRespiration in water occurs through the skin.\nColour change is a property of the skin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In a frog's heart, the ventricle opens into a sac-like structure located on the ventral side of the heart. This structure is the:","image":"","options":["Conus arteriosus","Sinus venosus","Vena cava","Gall bladder"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the ventricle opens into a sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart.\nThe sinus venosus is on the receiving side. It joins the right atrium and collects blood from the vena cava.\nThe vena cava are major veins that bring blood towards the heart.\nThe gall bladder is a digestive organ that stores bile.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the circulatory system of frog are correct?\n(i) The heart has two atria and two ventricles.\n(ii) The heart is situated in the upper part of the body cavity.\n(iii) The conus arteriosus lies on the dorsal side of the heart.\n(iv) The RBCs are nucleated and contain haemoglobin.\n(v) Lymph contains all the proteins and RBCs present in blood.\n(vi) Veins collect blood from different parts of the body and bring it to the heart.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the heart has two atria and only one ventricle.\n(ii) Correct: the heart lies in the upper part of the body cavity.\n(iii) Incorrect: the conus arteriosus lies on the ventral side.\n(iv) Correct: frog RBCs are nucleated and contain haemoglobin.\n(v) Incorrect: lymph lacks a few proteins and RBCs.\n(vi) Correct: veins return blood to the heart and form the venous system.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about control and coordination in frog:\n(i) The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord.\n(ii) Ten pairs of spinal nerves arise from the brain.\n(iii) Chemical coordination of organs is achieved by hormones secreted by endocrine glands.\n(iv) The fore-brain includes olfactory lobes, paired cerebral hemispheres and unpaired diencephalon.\n(v) The hind-brain consists of the optic lobes and medulla oblongata.\nWhich of the following options includes only the correct statements?","image":"","options":["(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(iii), (iv) and (v) only","(i), (iv) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the CNS is made up of the brain and spinal cord.\n(ii) Incorrect: the ten pairs of nerves arising from the brain are cranial nerves, not spinal nerves.\n(iii) Correct: hormones from endocrine glands bring about chemical coordination.\n(iv) Correct: this matches NCERT's description of the fore-brain.\n(v) Incorrect: the optic lobes belong to the mid-brain; the hind-brain consists of the cerebellum and medulla oblongata.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following and choose the correct option\nColumn I: A. Hermaphrodite; B. Direct development; C. Chemoreceptor; D. Blood gland in earthworm\nColumn II: i. Produces blood cells and haemoglobin; ii. Testis and ovary in the same animal; iii. Larval form absent; iv. Sense of chemical substances","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Hermaphrodite - ii: testes and ovaries are present in the same animal, as in earthworm.\nB. Direct development - iii: the young resemble adults and no larval form occurs.\nC. Chemoreceptor - iv: it senses chemical substances.\nD. Blood glands of earthworm - i: they produce blood cells and haemoglobin.\nThis gives A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i, which is option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The nasal epithelium of a frog functions as the organ of:","image":"","options":["Taste","Hearing","Touch","Smell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the nasal epithelium as the organ of smell in frog.\nIt is a cellular aggregation around nerve endings.\nTaste is served by taste buds and touch by sensory papillae.\nHearing is served by the tympanum together with the internal ear.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Oxygen dissolved in pond water passes into a submerged frog through its skin by","image":"","options":["Active transport","Osmosis","Diffusion","Phagocytosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that dissolved oxygen in the water is exchanged through the skin by diffusion.\nOsmosis is the movement of water, not of a gas like oxygen, so (2) is wrong.\nActive transport and phagocytosis are not the processes by which respiratory gases cross the frog's skin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following act as respiratory organs of an adult frog when it is on land?\nBuccal cavity, Skin, Lungs, Gills, Tympanum","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that on land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs act as respiratory organs.\nGills are not listed as respiratory organs of the adult frog.\nThe tympanum is an organ of hearing, not of respiration.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"A sperm formed in the testis of a male frog leaves the body. Which sequence correctly traces its path?","image":"","options":["Testis → oviduct → urinary bladder → cloaca → cloacal aperture","Testis → vasa efferentia → Bidder's canal in kidney → urinogenital duct (ureter) → cloaca → cloacal aperture","Testis → urinogenital duct → vasa efferentia → Bidder's canal → cloaca → cloacal aperture","Testis → vasa efferentia → urinary bladder → rectum → cloaca → cloacal aperture"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"There are 10–12 vasa efferentia. They arise from the testis and enter the kidney on its side.\nInside the kidney, they open into Bidder's canal.\nBidder's canal communicates with the urinogenital duct (the ureter) coming out of the kidney.\nThis duct opens into the cloaca, and sperms leave through the cloacal aperture.\nOption (3) reverses the order of the ducts. Options (1) and (4) wrongly include the oviduct, which is female, or the bladder and rectum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In an adult frog, how many digits end each forelimb and each hind limb?","image":"","options":["Five digits on each forelimb and four on each hind limb","Four digits on each forelimb and five on each hind limb","Five digits on each forelimb and five on each hind limb","Four digits on each forelimb and four on each hind limb"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the forelimbs of a frog end in four digits.\nThe hind limbs end in five digits. They are larger and more muscular than the forelimbs, and their digits are webbed.\nOption (1) reverses the two counts, which is a common mistake.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly traces the path of food through the alimentary canal of a frog?","image":"","options":["Mouth → pharynx → buccal cavity → oesophagus → stomach → intestine → cloaca → rectum","Mouth → buccal cavity → pharynx → oesophagus → stomach → intestine → rectum → cloaca","Mouth → buccal cavity → oesophagus → pharynx → stomach → rectum → intestine → cloaca","Mouth → buccal cavity → pharynx → stomach → oesophagus → intestine → rectum → cloaca"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The mouth opens into the buccal cavity. The buccal cavity leads to the oesophagus through the pharynx.\nThe short oesophagus opens into the stomach. The stomach continues as the intestine and then the rectum.\nThe rectum opens outside through the cloaca.\nThe other options misplace the pharynx, the oesophagus or the rectum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about reproduction in the frog:\n(i) The testes are attached to the upper part of the kidneys by a double fold of peritoneum called mesorchium.\n(ii) 10–12 vasa efferentia arise from the testes, enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal.\n(iii) The ovaries have a functional connection with the kidneys.\n(iv) The cloaca passes faecal matter, urine and sperms to the exterior.\n(v) Fertilisation is internal, but development is external.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iv) and (v) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the yellowish, ovoid testes are held to the upper part of the kidneys by the mesorchium.\n(ii) Correct: 10–12 vasa efferentia enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal, which leads to the urinogenital duct.\n(iii) Incorrect: the ovaries lie near the kidneys but have no functional connection with them.\n(iv) Correct: the cloaca is a common outlet for faeces, urine and sperms.\n(v) Incorrect: both fertilisation and development are external. Fertilisation takes place in water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In frog, the diencephalon is a part of the fore-brain.\nReason (R): Like the cerebral hemispheres, the diencephalon of frog is a paired structure.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: the fore-brain includes the olfactory lobes, the cerebral hemispheres and the diencephalon.\nR is false: NCERT describes the cerebral hemispheres as paired but the diencephalon as unpaired.\nSince R is false, options (1) and (2) are ruled out.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed by NCERT as a component of the lymphatic system of frog?","image":"","options":["Lymph","Lymph channels","Lymph nodes","Sinus venosus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the lymphatic system of frog as lymph, lymph channels and lymph nodes.\nThe sinus venosus is a triangular structure that joins the right atrium of the heart. It receives blood from the vena cava.\nSo the sinus venosus belongs to the blood vascular system, not the lymphatic system.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are components of the frog's lymphatic system?\nLymph nodes, sinus venosus, lymph channels, conus arteriosus, lymph, pericardium, vena cava","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymph channels and lymph nodes. That gives 3 components.\nThe sinus venosus, conus arteriosus and pericardium are associated with the heart.\nThe vena cava are major veins.\nAll four of these belong to the blood vascular system, not the lymphatic system.\nCount = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the frog, the thin-walled urinary bladder is located","image":"","options":["dorsal to the kidneys","anterior to the stomach","inside the pericardium","ventral to the rectum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT: the thin-walled urinary bladder lies ventral to the rectum, and it also opens into the cloaca.\nThe kidneys sit posteriorly, beside the vertebral column, so the bladder is not dorsal to them.\nThe pericardium covers the heart.\nThe region anterior to the stomach is occupied by the oesophagus and liver.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"When an adult frog is swimming under water, which structure acts as its respiratory organ?","image":"","options":["Lungs","Skin","Buccal cavity","Gills"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that in water, the skin acts as the aquatic respiratory organ. This is called cutaneous respiration.\nLungs and the buccal cavity serve as respiratory organs only on land, so (1) and (3) do not fit.\nGills are not respiratory organs of the adult frog described in this chapter.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The blood vascular system of frog is of the closed type, and blood is pumped by a three-chambered muscular heart.\nStatement II: The lymph of frog has exactly the same composition as its blood.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes the vascular system of frog as well-developed and closed. The heart is muscular, with two atria and one ventricle, and its pumping drives circulation.\nStatement II is incorrect. Lymph is different from blood because it lacks a few proteins and RBCs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The alimentary canal of a frog is relatively short. According to NCERT, the reason for this is that:","image":"","options":["Frogs absorb water through the skin and never drink water","Frogs are carnivores, so the length of the intestine is reduced","Frogs digest food only in the buccal cavity","Frogs lack a stomach and their oesophagus opens directly into the intestine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the alimentary canal of the frog is short because frogs are carnivores, so the intestine is reduced in length.\nAnimal food is easier to digest than plant matter, so a long intestine is not needed.\nOption (1) is a true fact about frogs, but it concerns water uptake and has nothing to do with gut length.\nOptions (3) and (4) are false. Frogs have a stomach, and digestion occurs in the stomach and the intestine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"To which class and phylum does the frog belong?","image":"","options":["Class Reptilia, phylum Chordata","Class Amphibia, phylum Chordata","Class Amphibia, phylum Arthropoda","Class Pisces, phylum Chordata"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that frogs can live both on land and in freshwater.\nThey belong to class Amphibia of phylum Chordata.\nThe class name reflects this dual life.\nReptilia is wrong because reptiles have dry, scaly skin, while frogs have moist skin.\nArthropoda is wrong because it is an invertebrate phylum, and frogs are vertebrates.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the frog, bile is secreted by the liver and stored in the gall bladder.\nStatement II: Bile contains enzymes that digest proteins in the duodenum.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the liver secretes bile and the gall bladder stores it.\nStatement II is incorrect. Bile only emulsifies fat; it does not digest proteins.\nIn the duodenum, carbohydrates and proteins are digested by pancreatic juice, which contains the digestive enzymes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the urinogenital system of frog is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Ten to twelve vasa efferentia arising from the testes enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal","In male frogs, the ureters serve as urinogenital ducts that open into the cloaca","In female frogs, the oviducts and ureters open separately into the cloaca","The ovaries are situated near the kidneys and have a functional connection with them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: 10–12 vasa efferentia enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal.\n(2) Correct: in males, the ureters act as urinogenital ducts that open into the cloaca.\n(3) Correct: in females, the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca.\n(4) Incorrect: the ovaries lie near the kidneys, but NCERT states there is no functional connection between them.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements comparing the limbs of a frog:\n(i) Hind limbs are larger and more muscular than forelimbs.\n(ii) Forelimbs end in five digits, hind limbs in four.\n(iii) Webbed digits of the feet aid swimming.\n(iv) The limbs help in swimming, walking, leaping and burrowing.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the hind limbs are larger and more muscular than the forelimbs.\n(ii) Incorrect: the digit numbers are reversed. Forelimbs have four digits and hind limbs have five.\n(iii) Correct: the webbed digits of the feet help in swimming.\n(iv) Correct: the limbs help in swimming, walking, leaping and burrowing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In frog, the spinal cord lies outside the cranium.\nReason (R): The medulla oblongata passes out of the brain box through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord, which is enclosed in the vertebral column.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the cranium encloses only the brain, so the spinal cord lies outside it.\nR is true: NCERT states that the medulla oblongata passes out through the foramen magnum and continues into the spinal cord inside the vertebral column.\nR directly explains why the spinal cord lies outside the cranium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the frog, partially digested food leaving the stomach is called chyme, and it passes into the duodenum.\nStatement II: In the frog, bile is secreted by the gall bladder and digests proteins and carbohydrates in the duodenum.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Chyme passes from the stomach into the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine.\nStatement II is incorrect on two counts:\n- Bile is secreted by the liver; the gall bladder only stores it.\n- Bile emulsifies fat. It is the pancreatic juice that digests carbohydrates and proteins.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly traces the path of sperms from their site of production to the outside in a male frog?","image":"","options":["Testis → Bidder's canal → Vasa efferentia → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca → Cloacal aperture","Testis → Vasa efferentia → Urinogenital duct → Bidder's canal → Urinary bladder → Cloacal aperture","Testis → Vasa efferentia → Oviduct-like sperm duct → Cloaca → Cloacal aperture (bypassing the kidney)","Testis → Vasa efferentia → Bidder's canal → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca → Cloacal aperture"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Sperms form in the testes and leave through the 10 to 12 vasa efferentia.\nThe vasa efferentia enter the kidney and open into Bidder's canal.\nBidder's canal connects with the urinogenital duct (ureter), which leaves the kidney and opens into the cloaca. The sperms then exit through the cloacal aperture.\nOption (1) reverses the order of the vasa efferentia and Bidder's canal.\nOption (2) wrongly sends sperms through the urinary bladder.\nOption (3) wrongly bypasses the kidney.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Sinus venosus\n(b) Conus arteriosus\n(c) Mesorchium\n(d) Bidder's canal\nList-II:\n(i) Double fold of peritoneum attaching testes to kidneys\n(ii) Triangular structure joining the right atrium\n(iii) Receives vasa efferentia inside the kidney\n(iv) Sac into which the ventricle opens on the ventral side","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Sinus venosus is the triangular structure that joins the right atrium and receives blood from the vena cava (ii).\n(b) The ventricle opens into the sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side (iv).\n(c) Mesorchium is the double peritoneal fold that attaches the testes to the kidneys (i).\n(d) The vasa efferentia open into Bidder's canal inside the kidney (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the digestive system of frog are correct?\n(i) Food is captured by a bilobed tongue.\n(ii) Chyme passes from the stomach into the duodenum.\n(iii) Final digestion of food takes place in the stomach.\n(iv) Undigested solid waste passes from the rectum out through the cloaca.\n(v) The oesophagus is a long, coiled tube.\n(vi) Villi and microvilli are finger-like folds on the inner wall of the intestine.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the bilobed tongue captures food.\n(ii) Correct: partially digested chyme passes to the duodenum.\n(iii) Incorrect: final digestion takes place in the intestine, not the stomach.\n(iv) Correct: solid waste moves into the rectum and out through the cloaca.\n(v) Incorrect: the oesophagus is a short tube.\n(vi) Correct: villi and microvilli absorb digested food.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are organ systems?\nStomach, Digestive system, Kidney, Respiratory system, Heart, Neural tissue","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Stomach, kidney and heart are organs: each is made of tissues arranged in a specific pattern.\nNeural tissue is one of the four basic tissues.\nThe digestive system and the respiratory system are organ systems, since in each two or more organs perform a common function.\nSo the count is 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Frogs are generally not seen during peak summer and peak winter.\nReason (R): During these periods frogs take shelter in deep burrows to protect themselves from extreme heat and cold.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that frogs are not seen in peak summer and winter.\nR is true. Frogs retreat into deep burrows, which is aestivation in summer and hibernation in winter, to avoid extreme temperatures.\nBecause they are hidden in burrows, they are not seen. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure in frog) with List-II (role):\nList-I\n(a) Walls of stomach\n(b) Pancreas\n(c) Liver\n(d) Villi and microvilli\nList-II\n(i) Absorption of digested food\n(ii) Secretion of bile\n(iii) Secretion of HCl and gastric juices\n(iv) Juice digesting carbohydrates and proteins","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The walls of the stomach secrete HCl and gastric juices, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) The pancreas makes pancreatic juice, which digests carbohydrates and proteins, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) The liver secretes bile, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) Villi and microvilli on the inner intestinal wall absorb digested food, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Mesorchium\n(B) Bidder's canal\n(C) Cloaca\n(D) Oviduct\nList-II\n(i) Small median chamber that passes faecal matter, urine and sperms to the exterior\n(ii) Duct arising from the ovary that opens separately into the cloaca\n(iii) Double fold of peritoneum that attaches the testis to the kidney\n(iv) Structure in the kidney into which the vasa efferentia open\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – (iii): The mesorchium is a peritoneal fold that holds the testis to the kidney.\nB – (iv): The vasa efferentia enter the kidney and open into Bidder's canal.\nC – (i): The cloaca is the common chamber for faeces, urine and sperms.\nD – (ii): The oviducts arise from the ovaries and open separately into the cloaca.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following structures of a frog:\nSkin, Lungs, Buccal cavity, Cloaca, Tympanum\nHow many of these act as respiratory organs when the frog is on land?","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Skin: yes. NCERT lists the skin as a respiratory organ on land.\nLungs: yes. They carry out pulmonary respiration.\nBuccal cavity: yes. It is the third respiratory organ on land.\nCloaca: no. It is the common chamber for passing faeces, urine and gametes.\nTympanum: no. It receives sound.\nSo three of the five structures are respiratory organs on land.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"A frog is found buried deep in a burrow in the middle of a cold winter, motionless and with a low body temperature. Which option correctly identifies the state of the frog and its main route of gaseous exchange?","image":"","options":["Aestivation; pulmonary respiration through lungs","Hibernation; cutaneous respiration through skin","Hibernation; respiration through the buccal cavity only","Aestivation; cutaneous respiration through skin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sheltering in a burrow during winter to escape cold is winter sleep, or hibernation. This rules out options (1) and (4).\nThe low body temperature is expected because frogs are poikilotherms.\nNCERT states that during aestivation and hibernation, gaseous exchange takes place through the skin. This is cutaneous respiration, not the lungs or the buccal cavity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the auditory apparatus of a frog can be seen from the outside?","image":"","options":["Pinna","External auditory canal","Tympanum","Internal ear"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A frog has no external ear.\nOnly the membranous tympanum is visible externally, on either side of the eyes.\nPinna and an external auditory canal are parts of an external ear, which frogs lack.\nThe internal ear lies inside the head and cannot be seen.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the frog, the duodenum receives both bile and pancreatic juice.\nReason (R): Bile from the gall bladder and pancreatic juice from the pancreas reach the duodenum through a common bile duct.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the duodenum receives both bile and pancreatic juices.\nR is true: both juices are delivered through a common bile duct.\nThe common bile duct is exactly how both secretions reach the duodenum, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In a frog, the lungs are located in the","image":"","options":["Head region, just behind the eyes","Upper part of the trunk region (thorax)","Posterior part of the body cavity, close to the cloaca","Posterior body cavity, on both sides of the vertebral column alongside the kidneys"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the lungs as a pair of elongated, pink, sac-like structures in the upper part of the trunk region, the thorax.\nThe frog's body has only a head and a trunk, and the lungs are not in the head, so (1) is wrong.\nThe structures lying posteriorly beside the vertebral column are the kidneys, so (4) is wrong.\nThe cloaca lies at the hind end of the body, so (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for the frog nervous system.\nList-I\n(a) Diencephalon\n(b) Optic lobes\n(c) Medulla oblongata\n(d) Cranial and spinal nerves\nList-II\n(i) Hind-brain\n(ii) Peripheral nervous system\n(iii) Fore-brain\n(iv) Mid-brain","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The diencephalon is the unpaired part of the forebrain, so it matches (iii).\n(b) The optic lobes characterise the midbrain, so they match (iv).\n(c) The medulla oblongata is part of the hindbrain, along with the cerebellum, so it matches (i).\n(d) Cranial and spinal nerves form the peripheral nervous system, so they match (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which is the most common species of frog found in India?","image":"","options":["Rana esculenta","Bufo melanostictus","Rana tigrina","Hyla arborea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT names Rana tigrina, the Indian bullfrog, as the most common frog species in India.\nBufo is a toad, not a frog, so it is the most tempting wrong choice.\nRana esculenta and Hyla arborea are not the species NCERT gives for India.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the case of animals, the term 'morphology' refers to the study of:","image":"","options":["Internal organs of the body","External appearance of the organs or parts of the body","Cells and their intercellular substances","The evolutionary trend in organ systems"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Morphology is the study of form, or externally visible features.\nIn animals, it refers to the external appearance of the organs or parts of the body.\nThe study of the morphology of internal organs is conventionally called anatomy, so option (1) describes anatomy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the blood and lymph of frog are correct?\n(i) Blood is composed of plasma and cells.\n(ii) The RBCs of frog lack a nucleus.\n(iii) Blood carries nutrients, gases and water to the respective sites.\n(iv) Lymph contains the same RBCs that are present in blood.\n(v) Platelets are one of the cellular components listed in frog blood.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT says blood consists of plasma and cells.\n(ii) Incorrect: frog RBCs are nucleated.\n(iii) Correct: blood transports nutrients, gases and water.\n(iv) Incorrect: lymph lacks RBCs.\n(v) Correct: NCERT names the blood cells as RBCs, WBCs and platelets.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"The brain of a frog is protected inside a bony structure called the","image":"","options":["vertebral column","cranium","pericardium","orbit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The frog brain is enclosed in a bony brain box called the cranium.\nThe vertebral column encloses the spinal cord, not the brain.\nThe pericardium is the membrane covering the heart.\nThe orbit is the socket in the skull that holds the eye.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the circulatory system of frog are correct?\n(i) Its RBCs are nucleated and contain haemoglobin.\n(ii) Lymph contains RBCs but lacks plasma.\n(iii) The heart has two atria and one ventricle.\n(iv) The renal portal system is the special venous connection between the liver and the intestine.\n(v) The blood vascular system is of the closed type.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: frog RBCs are nucleated and carry haemoglobin.\n(ii) Incorrect: lymph lacks RBCs and some proteins.\n(iii) Correct: the heart is three-chambered, with two atria and one ventricle.\n(iv) Incorrect: the liver–intestine connection is the hepatic portal system. The renal portal system connects the kidney and the lower parts of the body.\n(v) Correct: the vascular system is a well-developed closed type.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed as prominent endocrine glands of frog?\nPituitary, Thyroid, Liver, Thymus, Pineal body, Gall bladder, Adrenals, Gonads","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the prominent endocrine glands of frog as pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic islets, adrenals and gonads.\nFrom the given list, the endocrine glands are pituitary, thyroid, thymus, pineal body, adrenals and gonads. That makes 6.\nThe liver is a digestive gland that secretes bile, so it is not counted.\nThe gall bladder only stores bile and is not an endocrine gland.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In multicellular organisms, tissues organise into organs, and organs associate into organ systems.\nReason (R): Such organisation allows more efficient and better coordinated activities of the millions of cells that make up an organism.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the basic tissues organise into organs, which in turn associate into organ systems in multicellular organisms.\nR is true: NCERT says this organisation is essential for more efficient and better coordinated activities of millions of cells.\nR gives the reason why such organisation exists, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"During aestivation and hibernation, a frog stays buried in a deep burrow. Which organ carries out its gaseous exchange during this period?","image":"","options":["Lungs","Skin","Buccal cavity","Nasal epithelium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that during aestivation (summer sleep) and hibernation (winter sleep), gaseous exchange in the frog takes place through the skin.\nThe frog's skin is moist, smooth and well supplied with blood, so gases can diffuse across it.\nLungs are the tempting choice, but NCERT does not list them as the respiratory organ during these dormant periods.\nThe nasal epithelium is the organ of smell, not of gas exchange.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain of a frog?","image":"","options":["8 pairs","10 pairs","12 pairs","31 pairs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that ten pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain of the frog.\nTwelve pairs is the figure for reptiles, birds and mammals, so it is a common confusion.\nThirty-one is the number of pairs of spinal nerves in humans, not cranial nerves in frog.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Sinus venosus\n(b) Conus arteriosus\n(c) Bidder's canal\n(d) Nictitating membrane\nList-II:\n(i) Receives the vasa efferentia inside the kidney\n(ii) Triangular structure joining the right atrium\n(iii) Protects the eyes while in water\n(iv) Sac-like structure into which the ventricle opens","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Sinus venosus is a triangular structure that joins the right atrium and receives blood from the vena cava, so a-ii.\n(b) The ventricle opens into the sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart, so b-iv.\n(c) The 10–12 vasa efferentia enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal, so c-i.\n(d) The nictitating membrane protects the bulged eyes in water, so d-iii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about reproduction in frogs are correct?\n(i) The testes are a pair of yellowish, ovoid structures.\n(ii) The vasa efferentia enter the kidneys and open into Bidder's canal.\n(iii) In female frogs, the ureters also serve as the passage for ova.\n(iv) Development is direct, and the young hatch as miniature adults.\n(v) Fertilisation is external and takes place in water.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the testes are yellowish and ovoid.\n(ii) Correct: the vasa efferentia open into Bidder's canal inside the kidney.\n(iii) Incorrect: in females, ova pass through separate oviducts, not the ureters.\n(iv) Incorrect: development is indirect, through a tadpole larva that undergoes metamorphosis.\n(v) Correct: fertilisation is external and occurs in water.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about epithelia is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["They are sheet-like tissues that line the body's surface and its cavities, ducts and tubes","They have one free surface that faces a body fluid or the outside environment","Their cells are widely separated by an abundant intercellular matrix","Their cells are structurally and functionally connected at junctions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: epithelia are sheet-like tissues lining body surfaces, cavities, ducts and tubes.\n(2) Correct: each epithelium has one free surface facing a body fluid or the outside environment.\n(4) Correct: epithelial cells are structurally and functionally connected at junctions.\n(3) Incorrect: because the cells are linked closely at junctions, there is very little intercellular material between them. An abundant matrix is a feature of connective tissue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which tissues make up the human heart?","image":"","options":["Muscular and connective tissues only","Muscular and neural tissues only","Epithelial and muscular tissues only","Epithelial, connective, muscular and neural tissues"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the heart as an example of an organ that contains all four types of basic tissues: epithelial, connective, muscular and neural.\nThe heart is largely muscle, so options listing only muscle with one other tissue are tempting. They leave out tissues that are also present.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the excretory system of frog are correct?\n(i) The kidneys are compact, dark red, bean-like organs placed a little posteriorly in the body cavity on both sides of the vertebral column.\n(ii) Each kidney is made of several uriniferous tubules, which are its structural and functional units.\n(iii) In female frogs, the ureters and oviducts unite to form a common urinogenital duct before entering the cloaca.\n(iv) The urinary bladder is a thick-walled sac lying dorsal to the rectum.\n(v) Excretory wastes are brought to the kidneys by blood, where they are separated and excreted.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This matches NCERT's description of the frog kidneys.\n(ii) Correct. Uriniferous tubules, also called nephrons, are the structural and functional units of each kidney.\n(iii) Incorrect. In females, the ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca. A common urinogenital duct occurs only in males.\n(iv) Incorrect. The bladder is thin-walled and lies ventral to the rectum.\n(v) Correct. Blood carries the wastes to the kidneys for separation and excretion.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the lymphatic system of frog?","image":"","options":["Lymph","Lymph channels","Lymph nodes","Pericardium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Frogs have a lymphatic system in addition to a closed blood vascular system.\nThe lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymph channels and lymph nodes.\nThe pericardium is the membrane that covers the heart. It belongs to the blood vascular system, not the lymphatic system.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which description correctly matches the kidneys of a frog?","image":"","options":["Pair of compact, dark red, bean-like organs lying a little posteriorly on both sides of the vertebral column","Single, yellowish, ovoid organ lying in the upper part of the trunk","Pair of elongated, pink, sac-like organs lying in the thorax","Pair of loose, pale yellow organs attached to the stomach by mesorchium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the frog's kidneys as a pair of compact, dark red, bean-like structures. They lie a little posteriorly in the body cavity, on both sides of the vertebral column.\nOption (2) borrows features of the testes: yellowish and ovoid. The testes are paired, not single.\nOption (3) describes the lungs.\nMesorchium attaches the testes to the kidneys, not anything to the stomach, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Poikilotherm\n(b) Mimicry (as used in NCERT)\n(c) Aestivation\n(d) Hibernation\nList-II\n(i) Winter sleep in deep burrows\n(ii) Body temperature varies with environment\n(iii) Summer sleep in deep burrows\n(iv) Protective coloration that hides the frog from enemies","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Poikilotherm matches (ii): an animal whose body temperature varies with the environment.\n(b) Mimicry, in NCERT's usage, matches (iv): colour change for camouflage from enemies.\n(c) Aestivation matches (iii): summer sleep.\n(d) Hibernation matches (i): winter sleep.\nOption (3) swaps aestivation and hibernation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The lymph of a frog does not appear red like its blood.\nReason (R): Lymph lacks RBCs, which are the cells containing the red pigment haemoglobin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: NCERT states that lymph lacks RBCs, and that frog RBCs carry the red pigment haemoglobin.\nThe red colour of blood comes from haemoglobin inside the RBCs.\nWithout RBCs, lymph has no haemoglobin, so it is not red. A is therefore true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, why is the alimentary canal of the frog short?","image":"","options":["Frogs absorb water through the skin, so a long gut is not needed","Frogs are carnivores, so the length of the intestine is reduced","Frogs lack a stomach, so food passes directly into the intestine","Frogs are herbivorous tadpoles throughout life"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the frog's alimentary canal is short because frogs are carnivores. As a result, the length of the intestine is reduced.\nOption (1) is a true fact about frogs, but it is not the reason given for the short gut.\nOption (3) is wrong because frogs do have a stomach.\nOption (4) is wrong because the adult frog is carnivorous.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (body part of frog) with List-II (feature) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\n(a) Dorsal skin\n(b) Ventral skin\n(c) Tympanum\n(d) Nostrils\nList-II\n(i) Uniformly pale yellow\n(ii) A pair present above the mouth\n(iii) Olive green with dark irregular spots\n(iv) Receives sound signals","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Dorsal skin is generally olive green with dark irregular spots, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Ventral skin is uniformly pale yellow, so (b)-(i).\n(c) The membranous tympanum (ear) receives sound signals, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) A pair of nostrils lies above the mouth, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Setae help in locomotion in earthworm but are not uniformly present in all the segments. They are present in","image":"","options":["1st segment","Last segment","Clitellar segment","20th - 22nd segment"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: S-shaped setae are present in every segment of the earthworm except three regions.\nThese are the first segment (peristomium), the last segment and the clitellum (segments 14-16).\nSo options (1), (2) and (3) all lack setae.\nSegments 20-22 come after the clitellum, so they bear setae.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 7 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are ABSENT in the external body of a frog?\nNeck, tail, external ear (pinna), nostrils, nictitating membrane, tympanum","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","One"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Absent: neck and tail, since the body has only a head and a trunk.\nAbsent: the external ear, because only the tympanum is visible from outside.\nPresent: nostrils, which lie above the mouth.\nPresent: the nictitating membrane, which covers the eyes.\nPresent: the tympanum, found on either side of the eyes.\nSo three structures are absent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly traces the path of air from outside to the site of pulmonary gas exchange in a frog?","image":"","options":["Nostrils → Lungs → Buccal cavity","Buccal cavity → Nostrils → Lungs","Nostrils → Buccal cavity → Lungs","Mouth → Oesophagus → Lungs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that air enters through the nostrils into the buccal cavity and then passes to the lungs.\nOption (1) wrongly sends air to the lungs before the buccal cavity.\nOption (2) reverses the first two steps.\nThe oesophagus belongs to the digestive tract and does not lead to the lungs, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: The body of a frog is divisible into head and trunk, and a neck and tail are absent.\nStatement II: The hind limbs of a frog end in four digits, and the fore limbs end in five digits.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. The frog's body has only a head and a trunk. It has no neck and no tail.\nStatement II: Incorrect. The digit counts are reversed. The hind limbs are larger and more muscular and end in five digits. The fore limbs end in four digits.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (sense in frog) with List-II (sense organ):\nList-I:\n(A) Touch\n(B) Taste\n(C) Smell\n(D) Hearing\nList-II:\n(i) Nasal epithelium\n(ii) Tympanum with internal ear\n(iii) Sensory papillae\n(iv) Taste buds","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A Touch is served by sensory papillae, so A-(iii).\nB Taste is served by taste buds, so B-(iv).\nC Smell is served by the nasal epithelium, so C-(i).\nD Hearing is served by the tympanum with the internal ear, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly compares the fore limbs and hind limbs of a frog?","image":"","options":["Fore limbs have five digits; hind limbs have four digits and are smaller","Both limbs have five digits and are equal in size","Fore limbs have four digits; hind limbs have five digits and are larger and more muscular","Fore limbs have four digits and are larger; hind limbs have five digits"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the hind limbs end in five digits and are larger and more muscular than the fore limbs.\nThe fore limbs end in four digits.\nThe webbed digits of the feet help in swimming.\nOption (1) reverses the digit counts, and option (4) wrongly makes the fore limbs larger.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following open directly into the cloaca of a female frog?\nUreters, oviducts, urinary bladder, rectum, vasa efferentia, mesorchium","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ureters open into the cloaca. In females they do so separately from the oviducts.\nOviducts open into the cloaca separately.\nThe urinary bladder also opens into the cloaca.\nThe rectum opens into the cloaca, which passes faeces to the exterior.\nVasa efferentia are found only in males, where they arise from the testes, so they do not count here.\nMesorchium is a peritoneal fold in males, not a duct.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the brain of a frog, the midbrain is characterised by the presence of","image":"","options":["a pair of olfactory lobes","the cerebellum","a pair of optic lobes","the diencephalon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The frog brain has three divisions: fore-brain, mid-brain and hind-brain.\nThe midbrain is characterised by a pair of optic lobes.\nOlfactory lobes and the diencephalon belong to the forebrain.\nThe cerebellum belongs to the hindbrain.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of the urinogenital system of frog) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(a) Kidney\n(b) Urinary bladder\n(c) Ureter of male\n(d) Cloaca\nList-II\n(i) Small median chamber passing faecal matter, urine and sperms to the exterior\n(ii) Urinogenital duct\n(iii) Composed of uriniferous tubules\n(iv) Thin-walled, ventral to rectum","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Kidney – (iii): each kidney is built of uriniferous tubules, or nephrons.\n(b) Urinary bladder – (iv): it is thin-walled and lies ventral to the rectum.\n(c) Ureter of male – (ii): it carries urine and sperms, so it acts as the urinogenital duct.\n(d) Cloaca – (i): this small median chamber passes faeces, urine and sperms to the exterior.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nervous system of frog is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The autonomic nervous system consists of sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions.","The peripheral nervous system includes the cranial nerves and the spinal nerves.","The optic lobes and the cerebellum both belong to the hind-brain.","The central nervous system consists of the brain and the spinal cord."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the autonomic nervous system has sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions.\n(2) Correct: the peripheral nervous system is made of the cranial and spinal nerves.\n(3) Incorrect: the optic lobes characterise the midbrain. Only the cerebellum and medulla oblongata form the hindbrain.\n(4) Correct: the central nervous system is the brain plus the spinal cord.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In frog, which structures serve as the organs of touch?","image":"","options":["Taste buds","Sensory papillae","Nasal epithelium","Tympanum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists sensory papillae as the organs of touch in frog.\nTaste buds detect taste, and the nasal epithelium detects smell.\nThe tympanum works with the internal ear in hearing.\nSo touch is the only sense served by sensory papillae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following correctly describes the red blood cells (erythrocytes) of a frog?","image":"","options":["Enucleated and contain haemoglobin","Nucleated and contain haemoglobin","Nucleated and contain haemocyanin","Enucleated and lack any respiratory pigment"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the RBCs of frog are nucleated and contain a red pigment called haemoglobin.\nOptions (1) and (4) are wrong because frog RBCs have a nucleus.\nOption (3) is wrong because haemocyanin is not the pigment named. Frog RBCs carry red haemoglobin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a frog is in water, its lungs serve as the main respiratory organ and the skin only assists them.\nStatement II: The lungs of a frog are a pair of elongated, pink coloured, sac-like structures.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT states that in water the skin acts as the aquatic respiratory organ, through cutaneous respiration. Lungs are listed as respiratory organs only for life on land.\nStatement II is correct. This is exactly how NCERT describes the frog's lungs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A frog never drinks water.\nReason (R): A frog absorbs the water it needs through its moist skin.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT clearly states that the frog never drinks water.\nR is true: the frog absorbs water through its skin, which is always kept moist.\nThe frog does not need to drink because it takes in water through the skin. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A frog never drinks water and instead absorbs it through its skin.\nStatement II: The skin of a frog feels smooth and slippery because mucus is present on it.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the frog never drinks water and absorbs it through its skin.\nStatement II is correct. The skin is smooth and slippery because of mucus, and it is always kept moist.\nBoth statements are true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The alimentary canal of a frog is short.\nReason (R): Frogs are carnivores, so the length of their intestine is reduced.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes the frog's alimentary canal as short.\nR is true: frogs are carnivores. Animal food is digested more easily, so a long intestine is not needed and its length is reduced.\nNCERT directly links the two ('short because frogs are carnivores'), so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In multicellular animals, a group of similar cells together with intercellular substances that performs a specific function is called a/an:","image":"","options":["Organ","Tissue","Organ system","Organelle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT definition: a group of similar cells along with intercellular substances that performs a specific function is a tissue.\nAn organ is made of one or more types of tissues arranged in a specific proportion and pattern.\nAn organ system is two or more organs working together.\nAn organelle is a structure inside a cell, so it is below the tissue level.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are parts of the fore-brain of a frog?\nOlfactory lobes, cerebral hemispheres, diencephalon, optic lobes, cerebellum, medulla oblongata","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Forebrain parts: the olfactory lobes, the paired cerebral hemispheres and the unpaired diencephalon, which makes 3.\nThe optic lobes belong to the midbrain.\nThe cerebellum and the medulla oblongata belong to the hindbrain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"In the frog, food is captured with the help of the:","image":"","options":["Nictitating membrane","Copulatory pad","Bilobed tongue","Webbed digits of the hind limbs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the frog captures food with its bilobed tongue. The tongue is muscular and bilobed at the tip.\nThe nictitating membrane protects the eyes in water.\nThe copulatory pad is a male feature on the first digit of the forelimb.\nWebbed digits help in swimming.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During aestivation and hibernation, gaseous exchange in the frog takes place through the skin.\nReason (R): Frogs do not possess lungs and depend only on cutaneous respiration.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that during aestivation and hibernation, gaseous exchange occurs through the skin.\nR is false. Frogs have a pair of elongated, pink, sac-like lungs in the thorax and use them for pulmonary respiration on land.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about excretion in the frog is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Excretory wastes are brought to the kidneys by blood, where they are separated and excreted","A renal portal system connects the kidneys with the lower parts of the body","In females, the ovaries are functionally connected to the kidneys, so ova pass out through the ureters","The excretory system includes a pair of kidneys, ureters, the urinary bladder and the cloaca"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT says the blood carries excretory wastes to the kidney, where they are separated and excreted.\n(2) Correct: the renal portal system is a special venous connection between the kidneys and the lower parts of the body.\n(3) Incorrect: the ovaries lie near the kidneys but have no functional connection with them. Ova pass through separate oviducts into the cloaca.\n(4) Correct: these are the parts of the excretory system listed in NCERT.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-STRUCT-ORG-ANIMALS-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structural Organisation in Animals","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structural Organisation in Animals","type":"mcq","question":"Approximately how many ova can a mature female frog lay at a time?","image":"","options":["25 to 30","250 to 300","2500 to 3000","25000 to 30000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT: a mature female frog can lay 2500 to 3000 ova at a time.\nFertilisation is external and happens in water, so many eggs are lost. Producing a large number of ova makes up for this loss.\nThe other options are off by a factor of ten or a hundred.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 7 · Structural Organisation in Animals","difficulty":"easy"}]});
